# Every Behat step pattern the contexts declare, classified by what the feature suite does with it.
#
# The rule this file exists to enforce: the step vocabulary is an asset to spend, not inventory to
# audit. An idle step is an assertion somebody already built and nobody is making — a coverage signal,
# never dead code — so it must not be deleted for being unused, and a new near-duplicate phrasing must
# not be written before searching what exists. Both halves of that rule are claims about which steps
# are reached, and until this file existed nothing recomputed either: the prose stating them counted
# 205 patterns against 209, 47 features against 49, and named a context as wholly idle that eighteen
# scenarios were using. Every gate stayed green through all three.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <step pattern exactly as the attribute declares it> => used | idle | manual | refused
#
#   used    - at least one committed scenario reaches it.
#   idle    - no scenario reaches it. This is the coverage debt the rule is about: reach for these
#             when you touch a feature they fit. Adding one is fine and is meant to be visible.
#   manual  - a hand-invocation escape hatch, and NOT coverage debt: it prints to the formatter, or
#             overrides an automatic BeforeScenario hook. A committed scenario must never call one.
#   refused - a superseded phrasing kept registered so reaching for it hands back the canonical form
#             instead of "step undefined". It throws; it is not vocabulary that can be spent.
#
# The gate is BehatStepVocabularyGateTest (`make php.lint.step-vocabulary`). It fails when a declared
# pattern is classified nowhere, when a classified pattern no longer exists, when `used` is reached by
# nothing, when `idle` is reached by something, when a scenario calls a `manual` or `refused` step, and
# when a step written in a feature resolves to no pattern at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it deliberately does NOT do. Read this list as the specification of the gate's blind spots — a
# green build proves what is below the line, and nothing above it.
#
#   - It cannot judge a classification it derives. `used` and `idle` are recomputed from the tree, so
#     those two cannot be wrong; `manual` and `refused` are asserted only to be UNREACHED. Marking a
#     genuinely idle step `manual` would silence it as coverage debt and this gate would agree. Review
#     is the only control on that direction.
#   - It proves a pattern is REACHED, never that the assertion behind it can fail. A step whose body
#     asserts nothing counts as `used` here exactly like one that pins a real invariant.
#   - It does not detect near-duplicate phrasings. Two patterns saying the same thing in different
#     words both sit here as `used`, which is the split the rule warns about and the part still left
#     to a reader. What this file gives that reader is the list to search before writing a new one.
#   - It matches by re-implementing Behat's turnip syntax textually, not by asking Behat. A pattern
#     shape upstream adds that is not `:placeholder`, `(optional)` or a `/regex/` would be mismatched
#     — which is why every feature step must resolve to something: that check is what goes red first.
#   - It reads only `#[Given]`/`#[When]`/`#[Then]` attributes under `tests/Behat`. A step registered
#     any other way is invisible to it.
#   - It says nothing about whether a context is wired into `behat.dist.php`. An unregistered context
#     declares patterns nobody can call, and every one of them reads here as `idle`.
#
# Counts at the last edit: 101 used · 94 idle · 9 manual · 13 refused (217 patterns, 912 distinct
# step lines across the feature suite). They are recomputed by the gate, so they cannot drift here
# unnoticed — but nothing keeps this comment in step with them, so trust the file, not this line.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

